{"name":"Mulberry Purple","hex":"#4a3c62","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mulberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several trees, of the genus Morus, having edible fruits.","The fruit of this tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark purple color tinted with red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mulberry"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Morus (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Morus is a genus of flowering plants in the family Moraceae, consisting of 17 species of deciduous trees commonly known as mulberries, growing wild and under cultivation in many warm-temperate world regions. Generally, the genus has 64 subordinate taxa; although the three of most common are referred to as white, red, and black, originating from the colour of their dormant buds, and not necessarily the fruit colour, with numerous cultivars and some taxa currently unchecked and awaiting taxonomic scrutiny. Morus alba is native to China, but is widely cultivated and often naturalised across Europe, Southern Africa, South America, and North America; it is also the species most preferred by the silkworm. It is regarded as an invasive species in Brazil, the United States and some states of Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg/330px-Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morus_(plant)","wikidata":"Q44789"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"},{"title":"Mulberry (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The colour mulberry is a representation of the colour of mulberry jam or pie, a darker colour than that of the fresh fruit.\nTerms describing interchangeable shades, with overlapping RGB ranges, include burgundy, claret and maroon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Morus_alba_fruits.jpg/330px-Morus_alba_fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_(color)","wikidata":"Q218218"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/960px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbna